      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin acts as a critical amphiphilic stabilizer by packing its hydrophilic phosphate-glycerol head groups into the aqueous acidic phase while orienting its hydrophobic fatty acid tails into the oil to reduce interfacial tension. In your high-acid environment (pH 3.5), the charged head groups further optimize the electrostatic repulsion between emulsion droplets, effectively countering the destabilizing effect of the low pH. If phase separation is occurring, you may need to adjust the lecithin-to-oil ratio or consider adding synergistic salts to enhance this protective steric and electrostatic barrier.",
